The clinical and histopathologic findings are consistent with lymphatic thrombosis with recanalization. The presence of dilated and thrombosed vessels with signs of recanalization, along with positive immunohistochemical staining for D2-40 (podoplanin), which is a marker for lymphatic endothelial cells, supports this diagnosis. The patient's recent surgical history (excision of a nodule and axillary sentinel lymph node biopsy) provides a plausible etiology for post-surgical lymphatic injury. While Mondor's disease involves superficial thrombophlebitis of veins, the histopathology here is lymphatic, not venous. Axillary web syndrome is a fibrous contracture of the axilla, not associated with the described histopathology. Infectious or inflammatory lymphangitis would typically present with more acute inflammation and possibly systemic symptoms, which are not described here.

